{"name":"Ash in the Air","hex":"#d9dde5","fetched":"2026-10-16","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ash","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Abishira."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The solid remains of a fire.","The nonaqueous remains of a material subjected to any complete oxidation process."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ash"},{"term":"air","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Airoran."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The substance constituting Earth's atmosphere: a gaseous mixture of nitrogen, oxygen, and various trace gases.","The substance of the atmosphere seen as an agency of freshness."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/air"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ash","blocks":[{"text":"Ash is the solid remnants of fires. Specifically, ash refers to all non-aqueous, non-gaseous residues that remain after something burns. In analytical chemistry, to analyse the mineral and metal content of chemical samples, ash is the non-gaseous, non-liquid residue after complete combustion."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Wood_ash.jpg/330px-Wood_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ash"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Wood_ash.jpg/500px-Wood_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Wood_ash.jpg/1280px-Wood_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/09/Wood_ash.jpg/500px-Wood_ash.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wood ash","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AWood_ash.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0d/Air_pollution3.jpg/500px-A